# DURABLE REPRO — an f-string SUPPRESSES the call-site sigil rejection, and the
# accepted program then miscompiles.
#
# WRONG TODAY (measured at HEAD, Rust gg). The SAME call, two spellings:
#   print(abs(&a))          -> REJECTED, error[E_OwnershipMismatch]   (correct)
#   print(f"{abs(&a)}")     -> "OK: no semantic errors"               (wrong)
# and the accepted spelling then:
#   C backend    -> BUILDS, prints a raw pointer (e.g. 281474152536312)
#   LLVM backend -> llc HARD-FAILS:
#                     "'%v2' defined with type 'ptr' but expected 'i64'"
#
# So this is a Core #8 event — silent wrong output on one backend, hard build
# failure on the other, from a program `gg check` accepts — and the ONLY
# difference from the correctly-rejected spelling is the f-string wrapper.
#
# ROOT (already filed, but filed NARROWER than the truth): the f-string
# interpolation typecheck does `self.errors.truncate(saved_err_len)`
# (`typecheck.rs:1182-1186`), discarding every error raised while checking an
# interpolated expression. The existing TODO entry describes this as swallowing
# CARRIER-OP and operator-on-wrong-type errors (`f"{int ?? 5}"`,
# `f"{int.unwrap()}"`). **This fixture proves the blast radius is wider: it
# swallows OWNERSHIP/sigil-gate errors too.** Any diagnostic raised inside an
# interpolation is lost, so an f-string is a universal `gg check` bypass.
#
# ⚠ THIS IS WHY IT MATTERS FOR TRIAGE, not just for users: a probe that wraps
# its call in an f-string will report "accepted" for a shape the compiler
# actually rejects. That happened live — a gauntlet review concluded `extern`
# declarations do not reject the `&` sigil, when in fact they DO and only the
# f-string spelling was accepted. Never probe accept/reject through an
# f-string.
#
# INTENDED: both spellings REJECT with `E_OwnershipMismatch`. The f-string must
# not change whether a program is accepted.
#
# ⚠ The `extern` here is incidental — it is simply a callee with a resolved
# by-value signature. The same suppression applies to a user-declared function;
# `extern` is used so the fixture doubles as a pin that the extern path DOES
# reject when not wrapped.
#
# ORACLE (ggdef): OUT OF SUBSET — ggdef has no `extern` blocks. Recorded per
# Core #9.
#
# The seed is COMPUTED, not a literal, so no const-fold can elide the call.
#
# Cited from TODO.md (Semantics / reference-grade rejection — appended to the
# ROUND-16 T3 f-string blanket-suppression entry).

extern "C":
    int abs(int n)

int seed():
    return -5

void main():
    int a = seed()
    print(f"{abs(&a)}")
